    "title": "Pearl Masters MCX Refined: The Drum Kit Behind Relentless Reckless Forever",
    "content": "Released on February 11, 2011, *Relentless Reckless Forever* is Children of Bodom's seventh full-length album and the record that completed the band's commercial Nuclear Blast era with a signature blend of melodic aggression and keyboard-driven neoclassical songwriting. Recorded at Sonic Pump Studios in Helsinki with producer Anssi Kippo — the same environment that had defined *Blooddrunk* (2008) — the album delivered one of the most focused and confident drum performances of Jaska Raatikainen's studio career.\n\nFor Jaska Raatikainen, *Relentless Reckless Forever* represented the Pearl Masters era in its most refined state. The Pearl Masters MCX shells — an evolution from the Premium Maple configuration that had debuted on *Are You Dead Yet?* (2005) and been consolidated through *Blooddrunk* — brought a refined shell construction to the settled Zildjian A Custom and K Custom cymbal setup and the familiar Pearl Eliminator double pedal. The result is the most polished and controlled Pearl-era drum sound in CoB's catalog: every component understood, every performance deliberate.\n\nTracks like \"Shovel Knockout\" demonstrate the double-kick authority that Raatikainen had developed across six years of the Pearl endorsement, while \"Was It Worth It?\" showcases the dynamic intelligence behind the technical ferocity — melodic fill work that tracks the keyboard countermelodies with the precision that defines CoB's compositional approach. This article breaks down every component of Raatikainen's setup on *Relentless Reckless Forever* — the Pearl Masters MCX shells, the Zildjian cymbal configuration, the Pearl Eliminator, and the studio context that shaped the seventh CoB album.",
